# SAEA-E-003 "B-2a bias-list probe" (docs/readiness/2026-08-09-conec-
# evidence-shape.md "B-2a redesigned again"): does the FORM of a bias list
# matter at equal content, not merely whether biasing content is present.
#
# Four arms over the SAME ten earnings21 discovery samples (split
# r4-e21-sub10-sample, configs/dataset/r4-e21-sub10.json), all
# obs-agent-loop-ojw so results stay comparable with SAEA-E-002 (R4), all
# sharing ONE control arm (bare-core-ojw) so the three contrasts below are
# measured against literally the same control numbers.
#
# Exploratory, n=10, not powered for a precise estimate. Each contrast reports
# a paired exact Wilcoxon whose achievable two-sided p floor at n=10 is
# 2/2**10 = 0.00195 -- reachable in principle at this n, but still read effect
# sizes and direction alongside significance, not significance alone; ten
# samples is a probe, not a confirmatory run.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contrast 1
# unigram bag vs none (bias-conec-obsloop vs bare-core-ojw): the raw ConEC
# context list, capped at 100 single-token entries (bias_list_max_entries),
# extracted deterministically from the call's own ConEC context materials --
# largely redundant with R4 "matched" (same source, same transform family),
# kept here so it shares a control arm with the two contrasts below rather
# than costing a second, separately-run dose point.
# Hypothesis: a bag of call-relevant words reduces WER relative to no
# context, replicating R4 "matched" at this dose.
# A null result here (no gain over none) reproduces R4's own caveat: it is
# equally consistent with "context does not help" as with "unigram form is
# too fragmented to be useful" -- read together with contrast 2 below before
# concluding either.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contrast 2
# reconstructed phrases vs unigram bag (bias-phrases-ojw vs
# bias-conec-obsloop): the SAME source tokens, re-assembled into adjacent-line
# bigram phrase candidates (core.bias_lists.reconstruct_bias_phrases) instead
# of single tokens, capped on an approximate WORD-COUNT budget
# (bias_phrase_max_tokens=100) chosen to match the bag arm's own content
# volume (100 single-token entries = 100 words) rather than its entry count --
# see core.bias_lists.DEFAULT_BIAS_PHRASE_MAX_TOKENS for the exact arithmetic.
# This is THE open question B-2a exists to answer: form, not source or dose.
# Hypothesis: multi-word phrase candidates (e.g. "goldman sachs" instead of
# "goldman" / "sachs" on separate lines) improve entity-class WER over the
# fragmented unigram bag at equal content volume, because biasing toward
# multi-word proper nouns is the harder case the unigram format handicaps.
# INTERPRETATION LIMIT, measured 2026-08-10 on the real files before running:
# the reconstruction emits every adjacent-line bigram, and its quality varies
# strongly BY REGION within a file, because the ConEC list is a deduplicated
# DOCUMENT-ORDER token sequence (every count field is 1 in every file checked --
# 715/715, 811/811, 1057/1057 -- so it is not a frequency list at all). Roster
# and headline regions reconstruct real entities: 4397829 opens "january bed
# bath beyond mark tritton president ceo gustavo arnal cfo treasurer rafeh
# masood john hartmann", where the adjacent pairs ARE the participants.
# Boilerplate regions do not: 4330115 opens with webpage furniture ("investor
# kit news release print rss feeds receive email alerts view contacts
# printerfriendly version"), whose 50 bigrams contain about two plausible
# units. The 100-word budget takes whichever region comes first in document
# order, so the arms differ per sample in how entity-dense they are.
# Therefore: a null on this contrast means FORM is not load-bearing **for a
# list that mixes real multi-word entities with spurious adjacent pairs in an
# unmeasured proportion**. It does NOT establish that a clean entity list would
# fail. The clean-entity version of this question belongs to ladder rung L3
# (docs/readiness/2026-08-09-positioning-and-ablation-ladder.md sec. 3b), where
# the glossary is built from the system's own transcript and multi-word units
# are directly observable rather than inferred from adjacency.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contrast 3
# distractor-only vs none (bias-distractor-obsloop vs bare-core-ojw): the 769
# Fortune-500/CEO names present in the shipped Earnings-21 distractor list but
# ABSENT from the call's own content and from the reference-derived oracle
# list (negative control; core.bias_lists.distractor_only_terms) -- proves any
# WER change in contrasts 1-2 is not an artefact of merely supplying SOME
# capitalized word list, regardless of relevance.
# Hypothesis: no WER change relative to none (these names never appear in the
# audio, so they cannot correct a transcription).
# A null result here is the EXPECTED outcome and strengthens contrasts 1-2's
# reading; a NON-null result here (WER moves) would mean something in the
# pipeline reacts to bias-list PRESENCE regardless of relevance, and
# contrasts 1-2 would need to be re-read with that confound in mind.
#
# run_id:baseline_fragment
SAEA-E-003-b2a-none:bare-core-ojw
SAEA-E-003-b2a-unigram-bag:bias-conec-obsloop
SAEA-E-003-b2a-phrases:bias-phrases-ojw
SAEA-E-003-b2a-distractor-only:bias-distractor-obsloop
